Knuth & Dijkstra on the type enforcement question:

Dijkstra's position would be axiomatic: a program has a specification. The specification says error_pages maps codes in [100, 599]. If your type can represent 700 — a value outside the specification — then your program can be in a state that your specification says is impossible. A program is only provably correct when its state space contains no states the specification forbids. A uint16_t key with range [0, 65535] makes the program's state space strictly larger than the specification permits. That gap is where bugs live. He would demand a type whose construction fails on illegal input — making invalid states unrepresentable.

Knuth: more pragmatic, but equally rigorous on correctness. He would observe that C++ lacks native subrange types (unlike Pascal, which has them as a first-class feature). A wrapper type is the approximation. But he would also ask: where is the cost of the illegal state actually born? If the parser's parse_error_page_dir() immediately calls std::stoi and throws on out-of-range at the moment of parsing — with a line number — then the illegal value never persists. It is constructed and rejected in the same breath. He would consider this acceptable, provided the check is not scattered across multiple call sites.

The resolution for this codebase:
C++ has no subrange type. The options are:

uint16_t with validator enforcement — the illegal state can technically exist between struct construction and validation. Acceptable if the window is zero (it is — we throw immediately in parse_error_page_dir).
A wrapper type HttpStatusCode with a throwing constructor — eliminates the window entirely. Adds boilerplate.
Reject at parse time (in parse_error_page_dir) AND confirm in validator — layered enforcement, belt-and-suspenders.

Decision: option 3. Parse-time rejection means the illegal value never enters the struct. The validator confirms no path escaped. No wrapper type needed at this scale. Document this in the data model so the enforcement site is explicit.
The same logic applies to return_code. And return_code uses std::optional<uint16_t> — same reason as client_max_body_size in Location: "absent" and "present-but-zero" are ontologically distinct states. A sentinel 0 conflates them.